In article <3870C5EC.B24055D0@ecodev.com>, Scott Risdal <srisdal@ecodev.com> writes:

  Followups have been set to comp.sys.dec.

  As the original message is cross-posted to a variety of UNIX groups, 
  I will assume that the current and planned system will be running 
  some variation of Tru64 UNIX, Linux, or similar...

:We are planning to upgrade from our Alpha 3000 175 Mhz to a used Alpha
:800 5/500 Mhz machine with 256 MB memory...

  You will see a significant improvement in CPU and I/O performance 
  when moving from a DEC 3000 series EV4-based TURBOchannel box to 
  an AlphaStation 800 series EV56 PCI box. 

  A caveat that applies to most any system upgrade: make sure you are 
  upgrading your limiting factor(s), and that you are not upgrading 
  some aspect of overall system performance that isn't currently 
  limiting your performance...  You need to understand where your
  current system performance limits are: disk access, tape access, 
  CPU, I/O, physical memory, etc...  Put another way, if you are 
  currently limited by the performance of a disk spindle -- and 
  caching is not a factor or not applicable -- then CPU and memory 
  upgrades probably won't help...

:We use the machine as a
:development server for Web work as well as C++ compiling.  Generally
:there are 2-3 developers working on the machine via X(Hummingbird
:Exceed) on their PC's, with just one using the compiler...

  Given the intended use, I'd tend to recommend increasing the amount 
  of physical memory configured...  (X Windows and multiple users can
  both consume large tracts of memory for best performance...)  The 
  more physical memory you have, the more cache you can have, the 
  larger the working sets you can have, the more resident processes 
  you can have, etc...  Additional physical memory is often a cheap 
  and easy performance boost, in other words...

:We actually get by okay with the 3000, but it is a workstation class 
:machine and cannot take any more disks or tape drives and is a wee bit 
:slow on compiles...

  In the low- to mid-range, there are not particularly any in-built
  (in-grown?) difference between a workstation and a server, save for 
  (potential) differences in the I/O widgets configured -- and which I/O
  widgets have been tested in particular configurations...  A system that 
  is sold as a workstation may/will have a faster graphics I/O widget or 
  two, and a server may/will have faster/more I/O widgetry, but the basic 
  boxes are usually quite similar...

  You will also likely find it rather easier to locate PCI widgets than 
  TURBOchannel widgets, and the PCI is faster than the TURBOchannel...

:Any comments on the choice of the 800?  I am going purely on the
:recommendation of a salesperson, as I have yet to find any good source
:of comparison between the many models of Alpha.

  The AlphaStation 800, the AlphaServer 1200, the DIGITAL Ultimate 
  Workstation, the AlphaServer 4000, and the AlphaServer 4100 are 
  all members of the same general "Rawhide" platform "family"...

  There are general Alpha-related performance- and hardware-related
  information pointers included in the OpenVMS FAQ...

  nb: You might need to upgrade the operating system version as part
  of this upgrade, I'm not particularly familiar with the Tru64 
  UNIX version-platform requirements...

 --------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
   Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman#xdelta.zko.dec.com




