Loris Renggli <loris.renggli@sphys.unil.ch> writes:
> Daniele Fua wrote:
> > Anyone can suggest why the attached small sh script for pseudo random
> > number generation [0-99] is not able to keep the variable $rand outside
> > the scope of the while loop? Any suggested fix?
> >   ------------------------------------------------------------------------
> > #! /usr/bin/sh
> > #rand=`expr 1`
> > /bin/ps -e -o pid | while read mypid

The '|' forks a subshell for the while loop; variables can't get out
of the subshell back to the parent.  For this simple case, something
like

#! /bin/sh
#rand=`expr 1`
for mypid in `/bin/ps -e -o pid`
do
        if [ "${mypid}" != "PID" ]; then
                rand=`expr \( "${rand}" + "${mypid}" \) % 100`
        fi
        echo "inside loop: rand=" ${rand}
done
echo "outside loop: rand=" ${rand}

works fine, but you may have to be trickier if you really want to
process some input a line at a time.
